WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.934 --> 00:00:02.802
-Oh, my gosh. I'm so happy.

00:00:02.802 --> 00:00:04.637
Pump the crowd noise in now.
-Yeah, exactly.

00:00:04.637 --> 00:00:06.639
-I'm so happy that you're
on the show.

00:00:06.639 --> 00:00:08.841
I got to tell you, I'm a fan.

00:00:08.841 --> 00:00:10.543
I was screaming at the TV,

00:00:10.543 --> 00:00:13.146
so proud of you and proud
of our country,

00:00:13.146 --> 00:00:15.614
and I was seriously,
like, tears.

00:00:15.614 --> 00:00:19.051
It was like watching one of
those awesome sports movies,

00:00:19.051 --> 00:00:21.121
except it was real life.

00:00:21.121 --> 00:00:25.658
I could just imagine
what it was like for you.

00:00:25.658 --> 00:00:27.127
-Yeah, I mean, honestly,
I feel like it was

00:00:27.127 --> 00:00:29.362
a little bit the same for me
as it is for you,

00:00:29.362 --> 00:00:30.897
but, like, I have to keep it
together a little bit

00:00:30.897 --> 00:00:32.598
because I'm like,
"We're supposed to be here."

00:00:32.598 --> 00:00:35.502
And like, "We're doing the
winning thing, but like --"

00:00:35.502 --> 00:00:36.669
-Yeah.

00:00:36.669 --> 00:00:38.505
-It's like a childhood dream
come true,

00:00:38.505 --> 00:00:39.939
but then it actually comes true,

00:00:39.939 --> 00:00:42.075
and then you're like,
"Wait, what do I do now?

00:00:42.075 --> 00:00:43.542
No one ever talked about
the what do you do

00:00:43.542 --> 00:00:44.911
when you do it part."

00:00:44.911 --> 00:00:48.348
So then I just had as much fun
as possible, basically.

00:00:48.348 --> 00:00:53.119
-Oh, man, the world was watching
you, man, and rooting for you.

00:00:53.119 --> 00:00:56.055
Right now, you actually would
have been in Tokyo, correct?

00:00:56.055 --> 00:00:57.090
You would have been
at the Olympics.

00:00:57.090 --> 00:00:59.558
-Yeah, mm-hmm.
Yeah.

00:00:59.558 --> 00:01:01.060
-Bizarre times we live in.

00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:02.861
Are you prepping
for the Olympics

00:01:02.861 --> 00:01:05.598
for next year or not yet?

00:01:05.598 --> 00:01:07.000
-I mean...
-Too soon?

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:11.537
-Kind of.
Yeah, it's a little too soon.

00:01:11.537 --> 00:01:12.972
It's a really weird,

00:01:12.972 --> 00:01:16.709
like, training
sort of spot that we're in.

00:01:16.709 --> 00:01:18.677
It's like you can't really
go hard and stay

00:01:18.677 --> 00:01:21.247
at, like, your highest level,
because you can't do that

00:01:21.247 --> 00:01:22.815
all year round, and so --
-Yeah.

00:01:22.815 --> 00:01:24.684
-It's actually really bizarre.
We're used to having everything

00:01:24.684 --> 00:01:28.120
planned out in our lives
pretty much for years at a time.

00:01:28.120 --> 00:01:31.124
And so when the pandemic hit,
it was kind of like,

00:01:31.124 --> 00:01:33.059
well, are we just --
we're working out for health

00:01:33.059 --> 00:01:37.830
I guess. Got a Peloton,
doing yoga, doing Pilates.

00:01:37.830 --> 00:01:39.799
I'm just trying
to, like, stay healthy.

00:01:39.799 --> 00:01:41.467
And, you know, do all --
-Put on the Lululemons

00:01:41.467 --> 00:01:43.236
and go for it.
Yeah, yeah, yeah, you have to.

00:01:43.236 --> 00:01:44.838
-Yeah.

00:01:44.838 --> 00:01:47.607
-You're also known for activism
as well,

00:01:47.607 --> 00:01:49.842
which I really love about you.

00:01:49.842 --> 00:01:53.279
You're the first non-NFL athlete
to take a knee,

00:01:53.279 --> 00:01:56.148
and you got a lot of heat
for that one.

00:01:56.148 --> 00:02:01.354
And now I see a lot of sports
are supporting taking a knee.

00:02:01.354 --> 00:02:06.993
I know Major League Baseball
did it, WNBA did it.

00:02:06.993 --> 00:02:08.861
I think the NBA has a thing.

00:02:08.861 --> 00:02:12.365
But what was the shift
there you think?

00:02:12.365 --> 00:02:15.901
-I think the pandemic,
honestly, sort of laid bare

00:02:15.901 --> 00:02:18.404
a lot of the lies
that were told.

00:02:18.404 --> 00:02:20.406
We don't, oh, it's going to be
too much for healthcare,

00:02:20.406 --> 00:02:24.243
and too much --
well, we found like $3 trillion,

00:02:24.243 --> 00:02:25.544
like really easily.

00:02:25.544 --> 00:02:27.547
I was like, "Did we just have
this the whole time?

00:02:27.547 --> 00:02:29.782
Like, we just had
to, like, vote on it?"

00:02:29.782 --> 00:02:33.018
So I think people are kind of
starting to connect

00:02:33.018 --> 00:02:35.354
all of the different dots.

00:02:35.354 --> 00:02:36.956
I think we've seen
what's happened to Colin

00:02:36.956 --> 00:02:40.093
over all of these years
and how much backlash he's got,

00:02:40.093 --> 00:02:42.027
but then all of these things
come into fruition,

00:02:42.027 --> 00:02:45.331
literally what he's talking
about being played out,

00:02:45.331 --> 00:02:48.401
you know, for all of us
to see in these videos.

00:02:48.401 --> 00:02:51.437
I guess my hope is that people
are starting to connect the dots

00:02:51.437 --> 00:02:54.907
and realizing that we don't have
to live in a world like this.

00:02:54.907 --> 00:02:57.911
We don't have to live in a world
where, you know,

00:02:57.911 --> 00:02:59.845
we don't have healthcare
that's adequate

00:02:59.845 --> 00:03:02.048
and our schools
aren't adequate.

00:03:02.048 --> 00:03:03.616
We're one of the richest
countries in the world.

00:03:03.616 --> 00:03:05.684
Like, we can do better
for ourselves.

00:03:05.684 --> 00:03:09.155
And we can do better for the
people that are living here.

00:03:09.155 --> 00:03:11.524
-Is that what you're going
to talk about when --

00:03:11.524 --> 00:03:13.125
You're now a talk show host.

00:03:13.125 --> 00:03:15.094
You should have called me
for advice.

00:03:15.094 --> 00:03:17.330
Now we're now in the same club.

00:03:17.330 --> 00:03:21.334
This Saturday at 10:00 p.m.
on HBO.

00:03:21.334 --> 00:03:23.436
What do you talk
about on your show?

00:03:23.436 --> 00:03:24.670
What is it about?

00:03:24.670 --> 00:03:26.306
-It's called "Seeing America."

00:03:26.306 --> 00:03:30.743
I'm sort of on this mission
to, like, make politics cool.

00:03:30.743 --> 00:03:33.913
And I feel like politics
is always this thing,

00:03:33.913 --> 00:03:35.614
and it's sort of meant
to be like this

00:03:35.614 --> 00:03:38.083
or it's packaged as, "Oh,
this is not this cool thing.

00:03:38.083 --> 00:03:39.585
It's difficult to get
involved in,

00:03:39.585 --> 00:03:41.520
just, like, kind of stay
out of it."

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:42.722
And then people are like,

00:03:42.722 --> 00:03:44.190
"Well, I don't really
want to get involved."

00:03:44.190 --> 00:03:45.758
And it's like, actually,
politics is engaging with you

00:03:45.758 --> 00:03:47.460
whether you're engaging
with it or not,

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:48.861
all the time no matter what.

00:03:48.861 --> 00:03:51.598
Your whole life is political,

00:03:51.598 --> 00:03:53.366
especially if you're
a black person,

00:03:53.366 --> 00:03:55.835
or a black trans woman
or a gay woman like myself

00:03:55.835 --> 00:03:57.436
or a gay female athlete.

00:03:57.436 --> 00:04:00.506
You know, our lives
are political in so many ways.

00:04:00.506 --> 00:04:02.608
And so, trying to break it down
for people,

00:04:02.608 --> 00:04:04.510
to make it a little
bit more relatable

00:04:04.510 --> 00:04:07.246
and then get people energized
about getting involved

00:04:07.246 --> 00:04:10.015
in the civic process
and getting involved,

00:04:10.015 --> 00:04:13.953
and, you know -- just being more
active in their communities,

00:04:13.953 --> 00:04:15.355
but also for themselves
as well.

00:04:15.355 --> 00:04:17.456
Obviously, voting is a huge one.

00:04:17.456 --> 00:04:19.024
We're coming up on
the general election,

00:04:19.024 --> 00:04:21.627
which is, you know,
I mean,

00:04:21.627 --> 00:04:23.629
definitely in my lifetime
the most important one,

00:04:23.629 --> 00:04:27.300
but certainly a hugely
consequential election.

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:32.004
-Yeah, 2016, I think, 'cause
I was looking at the website,

00:04:32.004 --> 00:04:36.242
When We All Vote, and it said,
100 million people didn't vote.

00:04:36.242 --> 00:04:38.578
-100 million people didn't vote.

00:04:38.578 --> 00:04:41.781
-Did not vote,
so let's change that.

00:04:41.781 --> 00:04:44.016
It's free. You just
have to register

00:04:44.016 --> 00:04:45.451
and you have your voice heard.

00:04:45.451 --> 00:04:47.386
Why not do that?
-Yeah.

00:04:47.386 --> 00:04:48.854
I mean, When We All Vote
is amazing.

00:04:48.854 --> 00:04:52.124 line:0%
It's bipartisan, and voting
in general should be bipartisan.

00:04:52.124 --> 00:04:56.228 line:0%
We should try to be getting
as many people involved

00:04:56.228 --> 00:04:57.696 line:0%
in voting as possible.

00:04:57.696 --> 00:04:59.832 line:0%
You know, it's one
of the greatest things

00:04:59.832 --> 00:05:00.767 line:0%
about our country.

00:05:00.767 --> 00:05:02.234
We live in a democratic country,

00:05:02.234 --> 00:05:05.838
and so everyone can participate
and have their voice heard.

00:05:05.838 --> 00:05:07.340
You know, this is coming
from me,

00:05:07.340 --> 00:05:12.345
but there's a reason why certain
people or certain parties work

00:05:12.345 --> 00:05:14.913
so hard
to keep people out of voting.

00:05:14.913 --> 00:05:16.616
I think that the more
people vote,

00:05:16.616 --> 00:05:18.951
the more the policies
of the country

00:05:18.951 --> 00:05:21.320
reflect the people
that are voting

00:05:21.320 --> 00:05:24.356
and ultimately take care of
the people that are voting from,

00:05:24.356 --> 00:05:26.626
all the way from the presidency
down-ballot.

00:05:26.626 --> 00:05:28.294
In this pandemic in general

00:05:28.294 --> 00:05:30.596
we're seeing
how important mayors are

00:05:30.596 --> 00:05:32.832
and how important
the governors are.

00:05:32.832 --> 00:05:34.300
-Yeah.
-You know, it's like

00:05:34.300 --> 00:05:35.901
Gavin Newsom and,

00:05:35.901 --> 00:05:37.769
you know, the governor
from New York, Cuomo,

00:05:37.769 --> 00:05:39.939
they've become, like,
these superstars in this way,

00:05:39.939 --> 00:05:41.674
but it's like,
they're really important.

00:05:41.674 --> 00:05:43.542
They have a huge job.
And so all of these things

00:05:43.542 --> 00:05:45.244
affect our daily lives
every day.

00:05:45.244 --> 00:05:47.546
And so to get as many
people registered

00:05:47.546 --> 00:05:51.216
so their voice can be heard,
so the policies can reflect

00:05:51.216 --> 00:05:53.352
their needs and the needs
of their communities, I think,

00:05:53.352 --> 00:05:56.255
is the most important thing
that we can do right now.

00:05:56.255 --> 00:05:57.756
-Megan, before we leave,

00:05:57.756 --> 00:05:59.425
do you want to move your scarf
out of the way?

00:05:59.425 --> 00:06:02.061 line:0%
I don't know if it was
covering your shirt,

00:06:02.061 --> 00:06:05.364 line:0%
but I just thought it was pretty
important that you see it.

00:06:05.364 --> 00:06:06.498
-Yes.

00:06:06.498 --> 00:06:08.033
-Thank you so much
for coming on my show.

00:06:08.033 --> 00:06:09.434
I'm a big fan.

00:06:09.434 --> 00:06:12.104
Please come back, and I'd love
to see you in person.

00:06:12.104 --> 00:06:13.973
-Yeah, let's do this
in person again,

00:06:13.973 --> 00:06:16.742
and it's a great time
to arrest the cops,

00:06:16.742 --> 00:06:19.345
so, say her name,
Breonna Taylor,

00:06:19.345 --> 00:06:21.413
rest in peace,
you went too soon.

00:06:21.413 --> 00:06:22.782
-Megan Rapinoe, everybody.

00:06:22.782 --> 00:06:25.184
Check out whenweallvote.org

00:06:25.184 --> 00:06:27.285
for more information
on how to get involved.

00:06:27.285 --> 00:06:29.555
We'll be right back with
a performance from Angel Olsen.

00:06:29.555 --> 00:06:30.556
Stick around.

